DATA={'IV/12': {'intro': 'Winding numbers measure how closed curves wrap around points, while the argument principle converts winding into an exact count of zeros and poles. This chapter is the bridge from contour integration to global counting theorems.', 'sections': [('Index of a closed curve', 'For a closed curve $\\Gamma$ avoiding $a$, the index $\\operatorname{Ind}(\\Gamma,a)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma \\frac{dz}{z-a}$ records the net winding around $a$.'), ('Homotopy invariance', 'The winding number is unchanged under deformations of the curve that avoid the reference point.'), ('Local constancy', 'As a function of $a$ off the curve, the winding number is integer-valued and locally constant on each component of the complement.'), ('Logarithmic derivative', "For meromorphic $f$, the quotient $f'/f$ has residues equal to zero multiplicities and negatives of pole multiplicities."), ('Argument principle', "The contour integral of $f'/f$ counts zeros minus poles inside a contour, weighted by multiplicity."), ('Change of argument', 'The argument principle can be interpreted as total variation of the argument of $f(\\Gamma)$.'), ('Counting polynomial roots', 'Contours can count roots in regions without locating them explicitly.'), ('General residue formulation', 'The winding-number form of the residue theorem allows multiply wound contours and non-simple geometry.'), ('Boundary hypotheses', 'The argument principle requires the contour to avoid zeros and poles of the meromorphic function.'), ('Topological synthesis', 'Winding, residues, multiplicity, and image curves are different faces of the same integer-valued invariant.')], 'theorems': [('Integer-valued index', 'For a closed piecewise $C^1$ curve $\\Gamma$ and $a\\notin\\Gamma$, $\\operatorname{Ind}(\\Gamma,a)$ is an integer.', 'Locally choose a branch of the logarithm along successive short pieces of the curve. The integral of $(z-a)^{-1}$ is the net change of logarithm, and after one circuit the logarithm can change only by an integral multiple of $2\\pi i$.'), ('Argument principle', "If $f$ is meromorphic inside and on $\\Gamma$, with no zeros or poles on $\\Gamma$, then $\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma f'(z)/f(z)\\,dz=N-P$, counting zeros and poles with multiplicity in the simple winding-one case.", 'The logarithmic derivative has a simple pole at every zero with residue equal to its multiplicity and at every pole with residue minus its order. Apply the residue theorem.'), ('Homotopy invariance of index', 'If two closed curves are homotopic in $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{a\\}$, then they have the same winding number about $a$.', 'Apply homotopy invariance of contour integrals to the holomorphic function $(z-a)^{-1}$ on the punctured plane.'), ('Local constancy of index', 'The map $a\\mapsto\\operatorname{Ind}(\\Gamma,a)$ is constant on every connected component of $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\Gamma$.', 'The integral depends continuously on $a$ off the curve, while its values are integers. A continuous integer-valued function is locally constant, hence constant on connected components.')]}, 'IV/13': {'intro': "Rouché's theorem compares two holomorphic functions on a boundary and concludes that they have the same number of interior zeros. It is one of the most efficient tools for root counting and perturbative existence.", 'sections': [('Boundary domination', 'The hypothesis $|g|<|f|$ on a contour ensures that $f$ and $f+g$ never cross zero along the boundary.'), ('Homotopy viewpoint', 'The family $f+t g$ remains nonzero on the contour, so its boundary winding around zero cannot change.'), ('Zero counting', 'The argument principle turns that invariant winding number into equality of zero counts.'), ('Polynomial applications', 'Dominant monomials on circles can count roots inside disks.'), ('Perturbation stability', 'Small holomorphic perturbations preserve the number of zeros inside a protected contour.'), ('Localization', 'Choosing several contours can isolate different root clusters.'), ('Comparison choices', 'The art is to choose the part of the function that dominates on the chosen boundary.'), ('Multiplicity', 'Rouché counts zeros with multiplicity, exactly as the argument principle does.')], 'theorems': [('Rouché theorem', 'If $f,g$ are holomorphic inside and on a simple closed contour $\\Gamma$ and $|g|<|f|$ on $\\Gamma$, then $f$ and $f+g$ have the same number of zeros inside, counted with multiplicity.', 'For $0\\le t\\le1$, $f+tg$ is nonzero on $\\Gamma$ because $|tg|<|f|$. Hence the winding number of $(f+tg)(\\Gamma)$ about zero is constant in $t$. Apply the argument principle at $t=0$ and $t=1$.'), ('Polynomial root localization', 'If on $|z|=R$ one term of a polynomial strictly dominates the sum of all others, then the polynomial has the same number of zeros in $|z|<R$ as that dominant term.', 'Apply Rouché with the dominant term as $f$ and the sum of the remaining terms as $g$.'), ('Perturbative persistence', 'If $f$ has no zeros on $\\Gamma$ and $\\|g\\|_{\\Gamma}<\\min_{\\Gamma}|f|$, then $f+g$ has the same interior zero count as $f$.', 'The displayed inequality is precisely the strict Rouché hypothesis.')]}, 'IV/14': {'intro': 'Branches of logarithms and roots exist exactly when the topology of the domain allows one to choose arguments continuously. The obstruction is winding around the origin.', 'sections': [('Multivalued logarithm', 'Solving $e^w=z$ gives values differing by integer multiples of $2\\pi i$.'), ('Branches', 'A branch of logarithm on a domain is a holomorphic function $L$ with $e^{L(z)}=z$.'), ('Argument functions', 'Writing $L(z)=\\log|z|+i\\operatorname{Arg}(z)$ links branches of logarithm to continuous arguments.'), ('Slit domains', 'Removing a ray from the plane yields standard simply connected domains supporting logarithm branches.'), ('Nth roots', 'A branch of $z^{1/n}$ is obtained from a logarithm branch by exponentiation.'), ('Powers', 'For complex exponent $\\alpha$, define $z^\\alpha=e^{\\alpha L(z)}$ relative to a chosen logarithm branch.'), ('Monodromy', 'Analytic continuation around the origin changes the logarithm by $2\\pi i$ and roots by roots of unity.'), ('Integral criterion', 'A logarithm branch exists when the integral of $1/z$ around every closed contour in the domain vanishes.'), ('Topological obstruction', 'Nonzero winding around zero prevents a globally single-valued logarithm.')], 'theorems': [('Logarithm branch criterion', 'On a domain $D\\subset\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, a holomorphic logarithm exists if $\\int_\\gamma dz/z=0$ for every closed contour $\\gamma$ in $D$.', 'The vanishing integral condition gives a primitive $L$ of $1/z$. Then $e^{-L(z)}z$ has derivative zero, so after adding a constant to $L$ one obtains $e^{L(z)}=z$.'), ('Simply connected logarithm theorem', 'Every simply connected domain avoiding zero admits a holomorphic logarithm.', "The function $1/z$ is holomorphic on the domain, and Cauchy's theorem makes all its closed contour integrals vanish. Apply the logarithm branch criterion."), ('Root from logarithm', 'If $L$ is a logarithm branch on $D$, then $R(z)=e^{L(z)/n}$ is a holomorphic branch of the $n$th root.', 'Directly, $R(z)^n=e^{L(z)}=z$, and holomorphicity follows from composition.')]}, 'IV/15': {'intro': 'Analytic continuation extends local holomorphic data across overlapping domains. The identity theorem guarantees uniqueness, while singularities and monodromy determine how far continuation can proceed.', 'sections': [('Germs', 'A germ records the local behavior of a holomorphic function near one point, modulo agreement on smaller neighborhoods.'), ('Direct continuation', 'Two local representatives continue one another when they agree on a connected overlap.'), ('Chains of continuation', 'A function can be propagated through a sequence of overlapping disks.'), ('Uniqueness', 'The identity theorem prevents two different continuations from branching on the same connected overlap.'), ('Continuation along paths', 'Local elements can be transported step by step along a curve when singularities are avoided.'), ('Natural boundaries', 'Some power series encounter singularities densely along their circle of convergence and cannot continue through it.'), ('Singular obstruction', 'Poles, branch points, and essential singularities can stop single-valued continuation.'), ('Monodromy preview', 'Continuation around loops may return a different branch unless topology forces uniqueness globally.'), ('Maximal continuation', 'The collection of all continuations naturally suggests a Riemann surface rather than a single planar domain.')], 'theorems': [('Uniqueness of analytic continuation', 'If two holomorphic continuations agree on one nonempty open subset of a connected overlap, then they agree on the entire overlap.', 'Their difference is holomorphic and vanishes on an open set, hence by the identity theorem vanishes everywhere on the connected overlap.'), ('Continuation along a fixed chain is unique', 'Given a chain of connected overlapping domains and an initial germ, at most one compatible holomorphic element can occupy each successive domain.', 'Apply uniqueness of analytic continuation at each overlap inductively.'), ('Pathwise local uniqueness', 'Two continuations of the same initial germ along sufficiently close subdivisions of the same path agree wherever both are defined.', 'Refine the subdivisions to common overlapping neighborhoods and repeatedly apply the identity theorem.')]}, 'IV/16': {'intro': 'Möbius transformations are the automorphisms of the Riemann sphere generated by fractional linear maps. They preserve generalized circles and provide the elementary coordinate changes of global complex analysis.', 'sections': [('Fractional linear maps', 'A Möbius map has form $T(z)=(az+b)/(cz+d)$ with $ad-bc\\ne0$, extended naturally to infinity.'), ('Matrix representation', 'Scalar multiples of the matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$ define the same Möbius transformation.'), ('Composition', 'Composition corresponds to matrix multiplication modulo nonzero scalars.'), ('Three-point normalization', 'A unique Möbius transformation sends any ordered triple of distinct sphere points to any other such triple.'), ('Cross-ratio', 'The cross-ratio is invariant under Möbius transformations.'), ('Generalized circles', 'Lines and circles on the plane are circles on the sphere, and Möbius transformations map generalized circles to generalized circles.'), ('Elementary generators', 'Translations, dilations/rotations, and inversion generate all Möbius transformations.'), ('Fixed points', 'Fixed points solve a quadratic equation and organize the elementary dynamics of the transformation.'), ('Disk and half-plane maps', 'Specific Möbius maps give conformal equivalences between the unit disk and half-plane.')], 'theorems': [('Three-point theorem', 'Given distinct $z_1,z_2,z_3$ and distinct $w_1,w_2,w_3$ on the Riemann sphere, there is a unique Möbius transformation with $T(z_j)=w_j$.', 'Construct maps sending each triple to $(0,1,\\infty)$ using cross-ratio formulas, then compose one with the inverse of the other. Uniqueness follows because a Möbius map fixing three distinct points is the identity.'), ('Cross-ratio invariance', 'Möbius transformations preserve the cross-ratio of four points.', 'It suffices to check translations, nonzero scalings, and inversion, which generate the Möbius group; direct algebra verifies invariance for each generator.'), ('Generalized-circle preservation', 'Every Möbius transformation maps circles and lines to circles or lines.', 'Translations and similarities preserve both classes; inversion maps circles/lines to generalized circles by an explicit equation calculation. Use the generator decomposition.')]}, 'IV/17': {'intro': 'Conformal maps preserve angles locally and reorganize domains without destroying holomorphic structure. Holomorphic maps with nonzero derivative are conformal, and the Riemann mapping theorem gives a universal model for simply connected proper planar domains.', 'sections': [('Local conformality', "A holomorphic derivative $f'(z_0)\\ne0$ acts to first order as a positive dilation followed by a rotation."), ('Angle preservation', 'The argument of the quotient of tangent directions is preserved by multiplication by one nonzero complex derivative.'), ('Local inverse', 'A holomorphic function with nonzero derivative has a local holomorphic inverse.'), ('Conformal equivalence', 'A biholomorphic map gives an analytic equivalence between domains.'), ('Disk automorphisms', 'Automorphisms of the unit disk are Möbius transformations preserving the disk.'), ('Half-plane equivalence', 'The Cayley transform identifies the upper half-plane with the unit disk.'), ('Schwarz lemma', 'Holomorphic self-maps of the disk fixing zero are contractive, with a rigid equality case.'), ('Riemann mapping theorem', 'Every simply connected proper plane domain is biholomorphic to the unit disk.'), ('Normalization', 'Fixing one point and the argument of the derivative gives uniqueness of the Riemann map.'), ('Boundary caution', 'Interior conformal equivalence does not automatically provide regular extension to arbitrary boundaries.')], 'theorems': [('Holomorphic maps are conformal off critical points', "If $f$ is holomorphic and $f'(z_0)\\ne0$, then $f$ preserves oriented angles at $z_0$.", "The first-order expansion is $f(z_0+h)-f(z_0)=f'(z_0)h+o(|h|)$. Multiplication by the nonzero complex number $f'(z_0)$ rotates and scales every tangent direction by the same amount."), ('Schwarz lemma', "If $f:\\mathbb D\\to\\mathbb D$ is holomorphic and $f(0)=0$, then $|f(z)|\\le|z|$ and $|f'(0)|\\le1$, with equality at a nonzero point or in derivative only for rotations.", "Apply the maximum principle to $g(z)=f(z)/z$, with removable value $g(0)=f'(0)$. For every $r<1$, maximum modulus on $|z|=r$ gives $|g(z)|\\le1/r$ inside; let $r\\to1$. Equality invokes the maximum-modulus rigidity."), ('Automorphisms of the disk', 'Every automorphism of $\\mathbb D$ has form $e^{i\\theta}(z-a)/(1-\\bar a z)$ for some $a\\in\\mathbb D$.', 'Move the image of zero back to zero with a standard disk Möbius map. Schwarz lemma applied to the resulting automorphism and its inverse forces it to be a rotation.'), ('Normalized Riemann-map uniqueness', 'If two biholomorphisms $f,g:D\\to\\mathbb D$ send $z_0$ to zero and have positive real derivative at $z_0$, then $f=g$.', 'The disk automorphism $g\\circ f^{-1}$ fixes zero. By the disk automorphism classification it is a rotation, and the derivative normalization forces that rotation to be the identity.')]}, 'IV/18': {'intro': 'Schwarz–Christoffel transformations map canonical half-plane or disk domains to polygonal regions. The exponents in the derivative encode turning angles at polygon vertices.', 'sections': [('Polygonal target', 'A polygon is specified by ordered vertices and interior angles $\\alpha_k\\pi$.'), ('Derivative form', 'For a half-plane map with real prevertices $x_k$, the derivative has factors $(z-x_k)^{\\alpha_k-1}$.'), ('Turning angles', 'The exponent $\\alpha_k-1$ records the change of boundary direction at the corresponding vertex.'), ('Integration', 'The conformal map is obtained by integrating the Schwarz–Christoffel derivative and adding affine constants.'), ('Prevertices', 'Determining the prevertices is the accessory-parameter problem and is generally nonlinear.'), ('Normalization', 'Three real degrees of freedom can be fixed by half-plane Möbius automorphisms.'), ('Rectangles', 'Symmetry reduces rectangle maps to elliptic integrals.'), ('Slits and degenerate polygons', 'Limiting polygon configurations produce maps to slit domains and strips.'), ('Numerical construction', 'Practical Schwarz–Christoffel mapping often solves the parameter problem numerically.')], 'theorems': [('Angle-exponent relation', 'Near a prevertex $x_k$, a Schwarz–Christoffel map with derivative behaving like $(z-x_k)^{\\alpha_k-1}$ maps a half-neighborhood to a wedge of interior angle $\\alpha_k\\pi$.', 'Integrating gives local behavior $F(z)-F(x_k)\\sim C(z-x_k)^{\\alpha_k}$. The argument is therefore multiplied by $\\alpha_k$, sending the half-plane angle $\\pi$ to $\\alpha_k\\pi$.'), ('Sum of exponents', 'For an $n$-gon, $\\sum_{k=1}^n(\\alpha_k-1)=-2$.', 'The interior-angle sum is $(n-2)\\pi$, so $\\sum\\alpha_k=n-2$ and subtraction of $n$ gives $-2$.'), ('Affine freedom', 'Once the prevertices and exponents are fixed, two Schwarz–Christoffel maps with the same derivative factors differ by a nonzero multiplicative constant and an additive constant.', 'Their derivatives are proportional by the chosen normalization, so integration yields $F_2=A F_1+B$ with $A\\ne0$.')]}}
